  "packages": [
    {
      "slug": "literal-dress-code-compliance",
      "script": "Sneakers earned a write-up? My new manager loved rules more than results, so I followed every line after her business casual violation. Page fourteen said visible tattoos must be covered. Nobody told the warehouse crew; within a day, half looked recruited by a long sleeve cult. Page twenty two allowed personal calls only on the break room landline. At lunch, the whole floor queued for one beige phone like the last parking spot before a blizzard. Page thirty required safety vests at all times on the production floor—which technically includes the bathroom. I wore mine there. By the third day, corporate called: productivity had dropped nineteen percent. By the end of the week, the handbook quietly changed. My sneakers stayed on my feet, unwritten up, for good. Which rule broke first: Page fourteen, Page twenty two, or Page thirty?",
      "editor_note": "Rewritten to 139 words with a four-word question hook, every handbook number and outcome preserved, and the kicker tied to Pages fourteen, twenty two, and thirty."
    },
    {
      "slug": "signed-return-policy-refund",
      "script": "She returned WHAT? A woman visits our store every few weeks convinced the sign by the register is a legal document. This time: a blender used for months, no receipt, no box, chipped blade, full refund. I explained the actual policy required a receipt within thirty days. “The customer is always right,” she said louder, as if volume were evidence. So I printed the policy, highlighted the thirty day line in yellow, and asked her to sign that she'd read it. She signed without reading. Six weeks later, she returned with a different broken blender. I pulled her signed page from the file and slid it across the counter. She read her own signature beneath the policy she claimed didn't apply. Then she left—no refund, no word. Does “always right” survive a signed thirty day policy?",
      "editor_note": "Rewritten to 136 words with a three-word question hook, every timing detail preserved, and the signed thirty day policy used as the specific kicker."
    },
